<!-- §01 ─── The Problem ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The O(L²) Problem</div>
<h2>Full Attention Doesn't Scale to Long Contexts</h2>
<p>Modern LLMs routinely process 128K-token contexts for tasks like multi-document reasoning and repository-level code analysis. But standard attention scores every query against every key — O(L²) work. At 128K tokens that means over <strong>16 billion query-key dot products</strong> per layer, per forward pass.</p>
<p>The chart below shows how attention time explodes quadratically. At 8K tokens the overhead is negligible. By 128K it dominates end-to-end inference, making long-context serving prohibitively expensive at scale.</p>

<!-- Canvas: O(L²) growth chart -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">attention compute vs. context length</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">quadratic wall</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cost-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Sparse attention addresses this by skipping computation for unimportant query-block × key-block pairs. The question is: how do you <em>decide which blocks to skip</em> without (a) expensive preprocessing, (b) poor coverage, or (c) corrupting attention distributions?</p>

<div class="callout callout-purple">
  <strong>Key intuition:</strong> Only a small fraction of key blocks are truly relevant to any given query. In practice, skipping ~50% of blocks loses less than 0.5% of accuracy — because most attention mass concentrates in a handful of blocks.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §02 ─── Five-Dimension Tradeoff ─────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-tradeoffs" data-code="tradeoffs">
<div class="section-no">02 — Five-Dimension Design Space</div>
<h2>Why Every Existing Method Has a Blind Spot</h2>
<p>Dynamic sparse attention methods adaptively choose which blocks to compute based on the input. But through systematic analysis, the paper identifies five critical properties that determine effectiveness — and <strong>no prior method satisfies all five</strong>.</p>

<table class="results-table" id="props-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Method</th>
      <th>Preprocessing-free</th>
      <th>Global Evaluation</th>
      <th>Query Independence</th>
      <th>Pattern-agnostic</th>
      <th>Softmax Granularity</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="mn">SeerAttention</span></td>
      <td style="color:var(--red,#f87171)">✗</td>
      <td style="color:var(--green)">✓</td>
      <td style="color:var(--green)">✓</td>
      <td style="color:var(--green)">✓</td>
      <td><span class="mn">Block</span></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
