<p>The critical conflict: achieving <strong>global evaluation</strong> (seeing the whole sequence to find important blocks) while preserving <strong>query independence</strong> (not mixing attention distributions across different queries). XAttention gets global coverage by sampling anti-diagonal elements and summing across queries — but this mixes information from different queries, potentially corrupting the importance estimate. FlexPrefill stays query-independent but only looks at the <em>last</em> query block, missing patterns in earlier positions.</p>

<div class="callout callout-cyan">
  <strong>The deadlock:</strong> If you use multiple queries to estimate importance globally, you're mixing their distributions. If you use only one query, you lose global coverage. RRAttention escapes this by using one query per stride <em>per head</em>, rotating which query is used across heads.
</div>
