</div>

<p>This design intersects naturally with the two most common attention patterns in transformer models: <em>vertical patterns</em> (certain key positions matter across all queries) and <em>slash patterns</em> (attention forms diagonal bands). By systematically varying the sampled position, the round-robin strategy catches both types regardless of which head exhibits which pattern.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 ─── Three-Stage Algorithm ───────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-algorithm" data-code="algorithm">
<div class="section-no">04 — Three-Stage Pipeline</div>
<h2>Query Sampling → Importance Estimation → Block Selection</h2>
<p>RRAttention operates in three stages at each layer during prefill. Walk through them below.</p>

<div class="step-list">
  <div class="step-box active" id="step-0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Query Sampling with Head Round-Robin</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each stride interval [i·S, (i+1)·S), head h samples one query at position P(i,h) = i·S + (S−1−(h mod S)). The sampled set covers all positions across heads — no position is left unrepresented.</div>
      <div class="step-code">Q_sample[h] = &#123; Q[P(i,h)] for each stride i &#125;</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Stride-level Importance Estimation</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each (query stride i, key stride j) pair, aggregate all S key vectors within stride j by summation, then dot-product with the sampled query. Apply row-wise softmax over key strides to get normalized importance scores. Complexity: O(L²/S²) — S² cheaper than full attention.</div>
      <div class="step-code">I[i,j] = Q_sample[i] · sum(K[j·S : (j+1)·S]) / (S·sqrt(d))</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Top-τ Block Selection</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Aggregate stride-level scores to block level. For each query block, sort key blocks by cumulative importance and keep the minimal set whose scores sum to ≥ τ. Always retain all blocks for the final query block (protecting generation quality). The result: a sparse block selection matrix used for the actual attention computation.</div>
      <div class="step-code">keep blocks until cumulative_score &gt;= tau (e.g. tau=0.95)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span id="step-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 3</span>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §05 ─── Efficiency ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-efficiency" data-code="efficiency">
<div class="section-no">05 — 2.4× Speedup at 128K</div>
<h2>The Efficiency Advantage Grows with Context Length</h2>
<p>At short contexts (8K), all methods are fast — sparsity barely helps. The gap widens dramatically as context length grows. At 128K tokens, RRAttention achieves a <strong>2.4× end-to-end speedup</strong> over full attention, beating both FlexPrefill and XAttention.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">attention time vs. context length — Llama-3.1-8B</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">2.4× gap at 128K</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="timing-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>RRAttention's efficiency advantage comes from two sources: (1) the stride-level softmax reduces complexity from O(L²) to <strong>O(L²/S²)</strong>, and (2) the round-robin strategy avoids redundant computation through systematic query sampling. At S=8, that's a theoretical 64× reduction in importance-estimation work.</p>

<p>Pattern search overhead — the time to decide <em>which</em> blocks to compute — is reduced by <strong>18.2% vs XAttention</strong> at 128K, because RRAttention's stride-level aggregation requires fewer operations than XAttention's anti-diagonal scan.</p>

<div class="callout callout-amber">
  <strong>Practical note:</strong> All sparse attention experiments cover the <em>prefill stage only</em> — decoding uses standard dense attention for all methods. The speedup is most impactful for long-prompt inference (RAG, document summarization, code context), where prefill dominates total latency.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §06 ─── Results ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Benchmark Results</div>
<h2>Higher Accuracy at Higher Sparsity</h2>
<p>On the HELMET benchmark (7 task categories including RAG, long-doc QA, summarization, and synthetic recall), RRAttention achieves the best accuracy <em>and</em> the highest sparsity among dynamic sparse methods. This Pareto-dominance — more sparse <em>and</em> more accurate — is the main claim.</p>

<p>Results below for <strong>Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct</strong>, averaged across all HELMET tasks at context lengths 8K–128K (τ=0.95 for XAttention and RRAttention, γ=0.99 for FlexPrefill):</p>

<div class="results-bars">
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">Full Attention (0% sparse)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="100" style="background:rgba(100,100,120,.6)"><span>56.42</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">FlexPrefill γ=0.99 (38.6% sparse)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="99.3" style="background:rgba(6,182,212,.5)"><span>56.02</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">XAttention τ=0.95 (47.5% sparse)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="98.8" style="background:rgba(6,182,212,.35)"><span>55.74</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">RRAttention τ=0.95 (48.7% sparse)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="99.7" style="background:var(--purple)"><span>56.24</span></div></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>RRAttention scores <strong>56.24</strong> — above XAttention (55.74) while also achieving <strong>higher sparsity</strong> (48.7% vs 47.5%). It recovers 99.7% of full attention performance. FlexPrefill scores higher (56.02) but does so at much lower sparsity (38.6%), meaning it computes far more blocks for only marginally better accuracy.</p>

<h3>Video Understanding (Video-MME)</h3>
<p>On Video-MME with Qwen2-VL-7B-Instruct, RRAttention achieves <strong>67.7 average</strong> — above full attention's 67.8 at the conservative setting, and 64.3 vs 63.9 at aggressive sparsity. The gains are especially pronounced on medium and long videos (60+ seconds), where temporal dependencies span many frames and global evaluation matters most.</p>

<p>Generalization experiments on Yi-9B-200K and Qwen3-30B-A3B show consistent improvements, confirming the approach is architecture-agnostic.</p>
</section>

